Undoubtedly there are many more bugs/enhancement needed, but these are the big ones:


Terminal segfaults on exit
	This seems to have started happening when I merged Terminal's TerminalRoster code from
	Haiku.

Many keyboard keys don't work or don't work in all apps
	Arrow keys don't work in Terminal, but do work in StyledEdit.  Page Up/Down, function keys
	and control key combos (i.e. menu triggers) don't work.  Probably an SDL driver bug.
	See src/servers/app/drawing/SDLInterface.cpp

B_PULSE events are not sent
	Probably issue with the registrar in Cosmoe, which has hardly been tested at all.  This
	prevents Pulse from working, among many other issues.

Cosmoe requires root to run
	This would be a huge, fundamental change.  The tentative plan is to implement ports, threads,
	and semaphores inside a filesystem driver to make them global.  Making it a filesystem driver
	would expose them all as files, preserving the UNIX everything-is-a-file ethos, and gives us
	a global root-level API without having to change the kernel, which is a hard line for me.
	Not started.

Deskbar does not set up its "Start" menu
	Whatever triggers the Deskbar to fill out its start menu is not happening

File Open/Save Panels show no files
	File Open/Save panels come up when called, and otherwise act normally, but show no volumes
	and no files.  See "BVolume and BVolumeRoster need improvement" below for possible cause.

Limited Roster/Registrar support prevents Deskbar from showing the list of running apps
	The registrar has received minimal attention in Cosmoe, other then getting it to
	compile and run.  The app_server is compiled without Registrar support, and it does not run
	with Registrar support turned on.

entry_ref's only work if Name holds an absolute path
	Converting from a BEntry or GetNextRef fills this out correctly, but considering how
	extensively entry_ref is used, it's certain this is causing issues somewhere.  I don't
	know of a specific problem though.

Tracker runs and does not complain, but nothing shows on screen

send_data() and receive_data() use a static, hard-coded 512 byte area to pass information
	The current implementation was a quick hack to get menus working, since they use this
	functionality extensively. I've never gone back and coded it to allocate memory dynamically.
	See src/system/kernel/thread.cpp

Sudoku hangs when clicking a menu
	Even though menus seem to work in all other apps, the Sudoku app acts differently.  It
	hangs waiting for menu info (in receive_data/send_data) from the wrong thread.
	See (possibly) src/system/kernel/thread.cpp or src/apps/sudoku

BVolume and BVolumeRoster need improvement
	BVolumeRoster gets filled out, but it's surprisingly tricky on Linux to get a list of only the 
	volumes a user would typically expect to see on a desktop.  So you'll end up with say /swapfile,
	but not	your mounted USB drive, for example.  The BVolume and BVolumeRoster issues MAY have
	something to do with the empty File Panel windows too, but that's just a guess.  Maybe it thinks
	it can't find any valid drives?

Unit tests aren't even close to 100% passing
	I've copied some of the Haiku unit tests out to /src/apps/testharness to make them easier to
	work with. They are a pain to work with since determining which test failed is unnecessarily 
	time-consuming, even with my simplification of the system.  I know this will be hugely valuable
	in making Cosmoe more stable and compliant though.

Sometimes Cosmoe won't start up unless you run clean_shm.sh *not* as root
	This makes absolutely no sense, since all shared memory areas get allocated as root, but 
	for some reason running clean_shm.sh as your regular user is sometimes necessary to get Cosmoe going.
	If you run cosmoe.sh and the SDL window doesn't even pop up at all, run clean_shm.sh.  Possibly due
	to low shared memory limits on the system.

Cosmoe stubs out Haiku's BCatalog system for translating strings
	I think this would work fine if the Haiku catalogs were merged and installed, and the BCatalog
	(and friends) source was synced up with Haiku, but I just haven't seen this as a priority yet.

Changing the decor with setdecor causes the SDL window to disappear
	The underlying cause is unknown.  Could it be as simple as the Desktop wants to recreate itself
	when the decor changes, and the SDL driver doesn't handle that?

The BDirectory copy constructor does not seem to work
	Discovered when trying to use a BDirectory parameter in setdecor.  Changing it to BDirectory& made
	things work.  Probably due to our special additions to BDirectory for Cosmoe.